# Line endings: LF everywhere, on every platform.
#
# This repository byte-compares generated artefacts against freshly generated
# ones in several gates — the dataset and example regressions diff committed
# `*.expected` against a `bxp-cli` run, the expression corpus is fed line by
# line to a parser that rejects a stray CR, and the documentation drift guard
# diffs every generated page and fragment against a fresh render. Every
# producer writes LF on every host. So on a Windows checkout, the default
# `core.autocrlf=true` converts the committed copy to CRLF and each of those
# gates reports a whole-file difference that is nothing but line endings.
#
# This used to be handled with one rule per path, which meant every new
# generated file broke the Windows leg alone until someone remembered to add a
# line here — twice now. One default is the fix: the working tree is LF for
# everyone, so nothing has to be enumerated and nothing can be forgotten.
#
# Windows tooling is fine with it. Nothing here is a `.bat`/`.cmd`; the NSIS
# script and the PowerShell asset builder both read LF, and every editor in
# use this decade writes it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries. `text=auto` already detects these by their NUL bytes, but a
# mis-detected binary is a corrupted file, so the known ones are declared
# rather than guessed. (`.svg` is deliberately absent — it is XML, and belongs
# under the text default above.)
*.png  binary
*.bmp  binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip  binary
*.wasm binary

# The one text file whose CRLF is the point. This is a verbatim slice of the
# JHU COVID-19 time series, which ships CRLF, and the example exists to show
# that real-world exports convert cleanly as they arrive. Normalising it would
# quietly edit the source data the page claims to be showing, and would stop
# the example tree exercising the CRLF input path at all.
docs/examples/real-world/covid-wide-to-long/sample.csv -text
